[DONE] Combat or dangerous events that may need manual intervention are hard to spot right now. Perhaps in the UI, characters under attack/targeted by an enemy can flash red (subtly) to alert the player that there’s a fight or something happening.
    Built as the note below planned it, and the note was right on every count.
    `threatT` is stamped on the DEFENDER inside `applyDamage`, which every source of
    harm in the game already routes through — blades, arrows, bolts, spells and the
    turret alike — so one write covers all of them and there is no reverse scan.
    Two exclusions, both asserted: a blow from your own line does not raise it, and
    neither does a burn ticking down with nobody behind it (`a` is null there). The
    alarm is for somebody swinging at you, not for damage in general.
    The pulse is a CSS keyframe on `.port.threat` with a `prefers-reduced-motion`
    block that turns it into a steady border — no JS loop, because the bar is rebuilt
    on events and there is no frame hook to drive one from. The rebuilds ARE coalesced
    (`queueSquadRefresh`, 120ms): a real fight marks a dozen bodies inside one second
    and clears them inside one second, and each of those would otherwise have rebuilt
    every port in the bar.
    The measurement is taken off the real DOM after a real rebuild — a flag the bar
    does not read would pass a flag test and change nothing on screen.
    THE PLAN THAT TURNED OUT RIGHT, kept verbatim because it is the pattern:
    The place for it is `makePort` inside `refreshSquadBar` — it already
    builds one div per body and already varies the class on state (`.sel`, `.down`), so a
    `.threat` class alongside those is the whole of the DOM work. What it should key off is
    the harder question: `c.target` is who THIS body is fighting, which is not the same as
    being in danger. The field that means "something is swinging at me" is somebody else's
    `target`, so this wants either a reverse scan or a cheap `threatT` stamped on the
    defender when a blow lands (`applyDamage` already touches the defender every hit and is
    the natural place). Prefer the stamp — a reverse scan over `chars` on every squad-bar
    rebuild is the kind of thing that gets expensive with forty risen in the bar.
    Note the bar is rebuilt on events, not per frame, so a pulse wants CSS animation rather
    than a JS loop, and `prefers-reduced-motion` should turn it into a steady border.

